# FreeSail: the captain's orders for the Sardinian sea, Port Mahon to the corn of Bona
# (package 104). A short book: the frigate's own, the starter book not loaded.

# The departure: from off the mouth of Port Mahon south round the Isla del Aire, a league
# and a half off (package 101's departure).
standing order "weigh": at the anchor let go, if the distance to 39 52 N 4 19 E is under 2 miles then get under way on the larboard tack and steer S
standing order "off the mouth": at under way, if the distance to 39 52 N 4 19 E is under 2 miles then set plain sail; shape a course for 39 45.6 N 4 25.2 E
standing order "south-east of the Aire": when the distance to 39 45.6 N 4 25.2 E is under 1.5 miles then shape a course for 37 12 N 8 00 E

# Across the open sea south-south-eastward for the offing of Cape de Garde, a mark fourteen
# miles north-north-east of it and well clear: the current runs west from Cape de Fer toward
# the cape (Berard 1837, p. 80), which looks like an island from the west (p. 130), and two
# days' account may be leagues out (the second noon's observation moves it). The course is
# shaped each hour while the offing is far and the mark east of the cape is not yet near
# (package 101's lesson: every hourly course holds only while the mark beyond is far).
standing order "the course for Barbary": every hour, if the distance to 39 45.6 N 4 25.2 E is over 3 miles and the distance to 37 12 N 8 00 E is over 3 miles and the distance to 37 12 N 8 00 E is under 240 miles and the distance to 36 58 N 7 53 E is over 10 miles then shape a course for 37 12 N 8 00 E

# The gulf of Bona: from the offing she stands south for a mark three miles and a half east
# of Cape de Garde (low rocks a quarter cable off its east end, Berard p. 130), the course
# shaped every ten minutes as the bearings correct her account, then for the anchorage under
# the Fort Genois, closing it sounding, and anchors in fourteen fathoms (Berard 1837, p. 131).
standing order "off Cape de Garde": when the distance to 37 12 N 8 00 E is under 3 miles then shorten sail
standing order "the course for the cape's offing": every 10 minutes, if the distance to 36 58 N 7 53 E is over 0.8 miles and the distance to 36 58 N 7 53 E is under 12 miles and the distance to 37 12 N 8 00 E is under 10 miles and the distance to the anchorage of the Fort Genois is over 3 miles then shape a course for 36 58 N 7 53 E
standing order "round the cape": when the distance to 36 58 N 7 53 E is under 0.8 miles then shape a course for the anchorage of the Fort Genois
standing order "the course for the anchorage": every 10 minutes, if the distance to the anchorage of the Fort Genois is under 3 miles and the distance to the anchorage of the Fort Genois is over 0.4 miles and the distance to 36 58 N 7 53 E is under 3 miles then shape a course for the anchorage of the Fort Genois
standing order "at the anchorage": when the distance to the anchorage of the Fort Genois is under 0.4 miles then come to an anchor
standing order "the lead off Bona": every 10 minutes, if the distance to the anchorage of the Fort Genois is under 5 miles then heave the lead

# The yards and the sheets, the bearings.
standing order "trim to the course": at steady on the course, if the manoeuvre in hand is not hove to then trim sails
standing order "trim on a shift": when the true wind veers 1 point or backs 1 point and the manoeuvre in hand is not hove to then trim sails
standing order "tend the sheets": every glass, if the manoeuvre in hand is not hove to then trim the sheets
standing order "bearings": every glass, if the land is in sight then take a bearing of the land; take a fix
